==English==

===Etymology===
Back-formation from {{der|en|enm|part-takinge}}, {{m|enm|part-takynge|t=a sharing; partaking}}, a calque of Latin {{m|la|particeps|t=participating}}; equivalent to {{com|en|part|take}}. Compare {{m|en|take part}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|en|GenAm}} {{enPR|pär-tāk'}}, {{IPA|en|/pɑɹˈteɪk/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-partake.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{a|en|RP}} {{enPR|pär-tāk'}}, {{IPA|en|/pɑːˈteɪk/}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪk|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|par|take}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|||partook|partaken}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|formal}} To take part in an activity; to [[participate]].
#* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|text=Brutes '''partake''' in this faculty.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To take a [[share]] or [[portion]] (''of'' or ''in'').
#: ''Will you '''partake''' of some food?''
# {{senseid|en|archaic}} {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} To have something of the properties, character, or office (''of'').
#* '''c. 1620''', {{w|Francis Bacon}}, letter of advice to Sir {{w|George Villiers, 1st Duke of Buckingham|George Villiers}}
#*: the Attorney of the Duchy of Lancaster , who '''partakes''' of both qualities, partly of a judge in the court, and partly of an attorney-general
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2001|coauthors=David L. Lieber;Jules Harlow|title=Etz Hayim: Torah and Commentary|page=1107|text=The people are encouraged to perceive their liberation in historic terms and to trust that new events will '''partake''' of past glories.}}

